# **Echo Engine Design Document (EDD)**

# **Volume V – Rendering System**

# **Chapter 11 – Rendering Architecture**

---

> *"Rendering is not the engine. Rendering is a service that visualises the simulation."*

One of the earliest architectural decisions in Echo Engine was to refuse the traditional idea that **the renderer owns the game world**.

Instead:

* The **World System** owns the simulation.
* The **ECS** owns gameplay data.
* The **Asset System** owns resources.
* The **Scene Runtime** owns player context.
* The **Renderer** visualises the current state.

This distinction influences every rendering decision made throughout the engine.

Unlike many browser-based engines where rendering and gameplay become tightly coupled, Echo Engine deliberately treats rendering as an independent subsystem.

---

# 1. Rendering Philosophy

The Renderer has one job:

> **Convert simulation data into pixels.**

Nothing more.

It should never own:

* gameplay
* AI
* physics
* networking
* world hierarchy
* streaming
* assets

Instead it consumes data produced by those systems.

---

# 2. High-Level Architecture

```text
Gameplay

↓

Scene Runtime

↓

World System

↓

ECS

↓

Asset System

↓

Renderer

↓

GPU

↓

Monitor
```

Notice:

The Renderer sits near the bottom.

Not the top.

---

# 3. Why This Matters

Many engines eventually evolve into:

```text
Renderer

↓

Everything
```

The renderer begins loading assets.

Managing transforms.

Creating gameplay objects.

Owning cameras.

Controlling lighting.

Running animations.

Eventually every subsystem depends on rendering.

Echo Engine intentionally avoids this.

---

# 4. Rendering Pipeline

Each frame follows this conceptual flow.

```text
Begin Frame

↓

Collect Visible World

↓

Build Render Queue

↓

Visibility Tests

↓

LOD Selection

↓

Material Sorting

↓

GPU Submission

↓

Post Processing

↓

Present Frame
```

Each stage performs one coherent task.

---

# 5. Frame Acquisition

The renderer never asks

> "What objects exist?"

Instead it asks

> "Which objects should be visible this frame?"

This difference dramatically reduces unnecessary work.

---

# 6. Scene Integration

The Scene Runtime supplies

```text
Active Scene

↓

Camera

↓

Environment

↓

Render Settings
```

The renderer does not decide

which scene is active.

---

# 7. World Integration

The renderer queries the World System.

```text
Renderer

↓

World

↓

Loaded Sectors

↓

Visible Cells

↓

Entities
```

Only loaded world partitions are considered.

---

# 8. Matrix Spatial Pipeline Integration

One of Echo Engine's defining architectural choices is that the renderer **never computes authoritative world transforms**.

Instead, it requests final transformation data from the Matrix Spatial Pipeline.

```text
Renderer

↓

Matrix Spatial Pipeline

↓

Transform.js

↓

Final Transform Matrix

↓

GPU
```

The Matrix Spatial Pipeline—using `Transform.js`, `SectorID`, and `CellID`—remains the authoritative source of spatial information. The renderer consumes this data without owning or modifying the world hierarchy. This keeps rendering decoupled from simulation and preserves consistent spatial behaviour across AI, physics, streaming, and gameplay.

---

# 9. Camera System

The renderer receives the active camera from the Scene Runtime.

Example:

```text
Scene Runtime

↓

Camera Service

↓

Renderer
```

Possible cameras include:

* FPS Camera
* Cinematic Camera
* Debug Camera
* Photo Mode
* Future Replay Camera

The renderer only uses the supplied camera.

---

# 10. Renderable Components

The renderer searches the ECS for entities containing:

```text
TransformComponent

MeshComponent

MaterialComponent
```

Objects missing any required component are ignored.

This allows invisible gameplay objects to exist without render overhead.

---

# 11. Render Queue

Instead of rendering immediately,

Echo Engine builds a Render Queue.

```text
Entity

↓

Collect

↓

Render Queue
```

Benefits include:

* sorting
* batching
* instancing
* transparency ordering
* shader grouping

---

# 12. Visibility Determination

Every frame begins with visibility testing.

```text
Camera

↓

Frustum

↓

Visible Cells

↓

Visible Entities
```

Objects outside the camera are discarded before expensive rendering work begins.

---

# 13. Frustum Culling

The renderer performs frustum culling using spatial information provided by the World System.

Objects outside the camera volume never reach the GPU.

Benefits:

* fewer draw calls
* reduced GPU workload
* lower CPU submission cost

---

# 14. Occlusion (Future)

Future versions may support:

```text
Camera

↓

Depth Information

↓

Hidden Objects

↓

Skip Rendering
```

Because the World hierarchy already partitions space, integrating more advanced visibility techniques becomes easier.

---

# 15. Level of Detail (LOD)

Large worlds require scalable geometry.

Example:

```text
Distance

↓

LOD0

↓

LOD1

↓

LOD2

↓

Billboard
```

The renderer selects the appropriate representation based on distance and performance targets.

---

# 16. Materials

Materials describe **how** objects are rendered.

They do not own meshes.

Example:

```text
Mesh

+

Material

↓

Render Object
```

Materials are retrieved from the Asset System through asset handles.

---

# 17. Physically Based Rendering (PBR)

Echo Engine is designed around a modern PBR workflow.

Typical material inputs include:

* Base Colour
* Normal
* Metallic
* Roughness
* Ambient Occlusion
* Emissive

Using standard PBR principles ensures consistent lighting behaviour across the engine.

---

# 18. Lighting System

The renderer supports multiple lighting types.

Examples:

* Directional Lights
* Point Lights
* Spot Lights
* Ambient Lighting
* Future Area Lights

Lighting calculations remain independent of gameplay logic.

---

# 19. Shadow System

The renderer is responsible for shadow generation.

Possible stages:

```text
Light

↓

Shadow Map

↓

Lighting Pass

↓

Final Frame
```

Future optimisations include:

* Cascaded Shadows
* Shadow LOD
* Dynamic Shadow Distance

---

# 20. Animation Integration

Animation data reaches the renderer only after being processed by the Animation System.

For GPU-driven animation:

```text
Animation System

↓

Vertex Animation Texture

↓

Renderer

↓

GPU Vertex Shader
```

The renderer does not perform animation baking itself. Instead, it consumes the preprocessed Vertex Animation Texture (VAT) data generated by the Animation Baking System, enabling efficient instanced animation for large numbers of characters while keeping preprocessing outside the render loop.

---

# 21. GPU Instancing

Many objects share identical meshes.

Example:

```text
100 Trees

↓

One Mesh

↓

One Material

↓

100 Transforms
```

Instead of issuing 100 independent draw calls, the renderer uses GPU instancing where appropriate.

Benefits:

* Lower CPU overhead.
* Fewer API calls.
* Improved scalability.

---

# 22. Render Passes

The renderer organises work into passes.

Typical passes include:

```text
Shadow Pass

↓

Geometry Pass

↓

Lighting Pass

↓

Transparent Pass

↓

Post Processing

↓

UI
```

Separating work into passes simplifies future renderer evolution.

---

# 23. Post Processing

Post-processing operates after the primary scene has been rendered.

Potential effects include:

* Bloom
* Tone Mapping
* Motion Blur
* Depth of Field
* Colour Grading
* Screen-Space Ambient Occlusion
* Anti-Aliasing

Each effect is optional and can be enabled or disabled according to hardware capability.

---

# 24. Hardware-Adaptive Rendering

One of Echo Engine's defining long-term goals is automatic adaptation to the user's hardware.

The renderer is expected to scale features such as:

* Shadow quality.
* Lighting complexity.
* LOD distances.
* Post-processing.
* Render resolution.
* Texture quality.
* Effect quality.

This adaptive approach aims to maximise visual quality while respecting the capabilities of the host system, rather than relying solely on manual graphics settings.

---

# 25. GPU Resource Management

The renderer does not load GPU resources directly.

Instead:

```text
Renderer

↓

AssetHandle

↓

AssetManager

↓

Runtime Resource
```

This preserves the ownership model established by D014.

---

# 26. Future Renderer Evolution

The rendering architecture has been designed to accommodate future enhancements without changing its core responsibilities.

Potential developments include:

* Render Graph.
* Deferred Rendering.
* Forward+ Rendering.
* Clustered Lighting.
* GPU-Driven Rendering.
* Mesh Shaders (where platform support exists).
* Bindless resource techniques as browser APIs evolve.

Because rendering remains decoupled from simulation, these improvements can be introduced incrementally.

---

# 27. Architectural Comparison

| Traditional Browser Engine             | Echo Engine                                       |
| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Renderer owns scene graph              | Renderer consumes World data                      |
| Renderer computes world organisation   | Matrix Spatial Pipeline owns transforms           |
| Renderer may load resources            | Asset System owns resources                       |
| Renderer often controls cameras        | Scene Runtime provides active camera              |
| Animation often tied to render objects | Animation System supplies VAT or skeletal results |
| Rendering tightly coupled to gameplay  | Rendering remains a visualisation service         |

---

# 28. Chapter Summary

The Rendering Architecture establishes a clear separation between simulation and visualisation.

Its key principles are:

* The renderer visualises; it does not own gameplay.
* Spatial information is provided by the Matrix Spatial Pipeline.
* Assets are supplied through the Asset System.
* Cameras and environment are controlled by the Scene Runtime.
* ECS provides renderable component data.
* GPU-friendly techniques such as instancing and VAT playback reduce rendering overhead.
* Hardware-adaptive rendering allows the engine to scale gracefully across a wide range of systems.

By treating rendering as a consumer of engine state rather than the owner of it, Echo Engine maintains strong architectural boundaries while providing a flexible foundation for future rendering technologies.

---

