# **Echo Engine Design Document (EDD)**

# **Volume XIII – Editor & Development Tools**

# **Chapter 20 – Editor Architecture & Developer Toolchain**

---

> *"A professional engine is judged as much by its tools as by its runtime."*

A game engine is far more than a renderer or a simulation framework.

Without effective development tools, even the most advanced runtime becomes difficult to use, maintain, and extend.

Echo Engine therefore treats its editor and developer tooling as **first-class engineering systems**, not optional utilities.

The runtime executes the game.

The editor creates it.

Both evolve together while remaining architecturally independent.

---

# 1. Tooling Philosophy

The Editor exists to improve developer productivity.

Its responsibilities include:

* creating worlds,
* editing assets,
* debugging gameplay,
* profiling performance,
* packaging projects,
* inspecting runtime data.

It does **not** become part of the runtime shipped to players.

The runtime and editor remain separate applications built upon shared engine libraries.

---

# 2. High-Level Architecture

```text
Echo Engine Runtime

↑

Shared Engine Libraries

↓

Echo Editor

↓

Developer Tools
```

Shared systems include:

* ECS
* Asset System
* Renderer
* World System
* Physics
* Audio
* Animation
* Streaming

The editor reuses engine functionality without duplicating implementation.

---

# 3. Core Components

The Editor consists of:

```text
EditorApplication

ProjectManager

AssetBrowser

WorldEditor

Inspector

HierarchyPanel

Viewport

Console

Profiler

BuildManager

PackageManager

PluginManager
```

Each tool has one clearly defined responsibility.

---

# 4. Editor Application

The Editor Application coordinates the complete development environment.

Responsibilities include:

* project loading,
* workspace management,
* tool coordination,
* editor preferences,
* plugin loading,
* runtime communication.

It does not contain gameplay logic.

---

# 5. Project Manager

The Project Manager handles:

* creating projects,
* opening projects,
* project settings,
* engine version compatibility,
* workspace configuration.

